<figure class="service-guide" aria-labelledby="service-guide-title">
  <figcaption>
    <span class="content-kicker">Service and entry-point atlas</span>
    <h3 id="service-guide-title">Choose the frontend by who must reach it</h3>
    <p>A Service gives a changing backend set a stable way to be found. The Service type changes the frontend and exposure mechanism; Ingress and Gateway API add protocol-aware routing rather than becoming new Service types.</p>
  </figcaption>

  <div class="service-cards">
    <article>
      <header><code>ClusterIP</code><span>default</span></header>
      <div class="mini-flow"><b>Pod client</b><i>→</i><b>VIP :80</b><i>→</i><b>Pod :8080</b></div>
      <p><strong>Use for:</strong> stable internal application access.</p>
      <small>The Service data plane chooses an eligible endpoint. The VIP is not an application process.</small>
    </article>
    <article>
      <header><code>Headless</code><span>clusterIP: None</span></header>
      <div class="mini-flow"><b>DNS query</b><i>→</i><b>Pod IP set</b><i>→</i><b>client chooses</b></div>
      <p><strong>Use for:</strong> peer discovery and clients that need individual endpoint addresses.</p>
      <small>No virtual-IP load balancing. Clients must handle churn, selection, and cached answers.</small>
    </article>
    <article>
      <header><code>NodePort</code><span>node IP + port</span></header>
      <div class="mini-flow"><b>client</b><i>→</i><b>Node :30xxx</b><i>→</i><b>endpoint</b></div>
      <p><strong>Use for:</strong> simple exposure or an implementation’s load-balancer target.</p>
      <small>Node reachability, firewall rules, traffic policy, and source translation all matter.</small>
    </article>
    <article>
      <header><code>LoadBalancer</code><span>controller required</span></header>
      <div class="mini-flow"><b>external IP</b><i>→</i><b>provider LB</b><i>→</i><b>Service / Pods</b></div>
      <p><strong>Use for:</strong> one externally reachable TCP or UDP service.</p>
      <small>The implementation decides whether it targets Nodes, NodePorts, or Pods directly.</small>
    </article>
    <article>
      <header><code>ExternalName</code><span>DNS CNAME</span></header>
      <div class="mini-flow"><b>Service name</b><i>⇢</i><b>external DNS name</b></div>
      <p><strong>Use for:</strong> a Kubernetes-local alias to a DNS name you manage elsewhere.</p>
      <small>No proxy, health check, ClusterIP, or EndpointSlice backend is created.</small>
    </article>
  </div>

  <section class="north-south-choice" aria-labelledby="north-south-choice-title">
    <header><span>HTTP and HTTPS entry</span><h4 id="north-south-choice-title">Add a routing API when many routes share entry infrastructure</h4></header>
    <div class="entry-flow">
      <div><small>Clients</small><b>api.example.com</b><code>HTTPS :443</code></div><i>→</i>
      <div class="control"><small>Gateway / Ingress controller</small><b>TLS + host/path matching</b><code>status → programmed data plane</code></div><i>→</i>
      <div><small>Route backend</small><b>Service port</b><code>→ eligible endpoint</code></div>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Prefer Gateway API for new, multi-team routing designs.</strong> Ingress remains common and stable but is frozen. A resource object does not carry traffic by itself; an implementation must accept it and program a reachable data plane.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="choice-grid" aria-label="Service selection quick reference">
    <div><span>Only callers inside the cluster?</span><strong>ClusterIP</strong></div>
    <div><span>Clients need Pod addresses?</span><strong>Headless</strong></div>
    <div><span>One external L4 service?</span><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></div>
    <div><span>Shared HTTP entry and routing?</span><strong>Gateway API</strong></div>
    <div><span>Just alias an external DNS name?</span><strong>ExternalName</strong></div>
  </div>
</figure>

